<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Santal people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Santhal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Santal are one of the largest Adivasi peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>, living mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jharkhand">Jharkhand</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, with communities in Bangladesh and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>. They speak Santali, a Munda language with its own Ol Chiki script. In 1855–1856 the Santal rebellion against landlords, moneylenders and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> led to the creation of the Santal Parganas district.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/santal-people.jpg" alt="Santal people" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ramjit Tudu · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baha_parab_4.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
